## 4. What blocks 0.99 on text, measured (12 October 2026)

- **The codec allows it, the outlines do not.** Exact outlines encoded like the reference and read back: hard IoU 0.991 (Four secure elements, 105 px), 0.997 (No worries.), 0.998 (Smoother. Clearer.), 0.998 (Bundled with OneKey Pro 2, small), 0.994 (device unlock line), 0.975 (100%, 11 px tall). Soft IoU ceiling 0.958 to 0.992. So 0.99 hard is reachable for every caption and 0.99 soft only for the large ones; the small UI texts cannot reach 0.99 soft against this video whatever the outlines.
- **The fitter is not the limit.** On the decoded synthetic frame with the true font the loop reaches 0.988 soft and 0.986 hard (size 104.875 for a truth of 104.860); with a wrong font (Plus Jakarta Sans) it stops at 0.900 (`F3-ceiling-four-secure.json`, `F3-ceiling-four-secure-red.json`).
- **The residual is glyph shape.** The residual map of Four secure elements (best version) concentrates on terminal cuts of s, c, e, r and on the F (narrower bar, stem 1 px left), not on placement or blur: outline differences between the OneKey typeface and every free family. The F1 creator moves points along normals with smoothing and a step cap; longer snapping (200 iterations, step 0.02 em) gave no gain (V_010, V_011), so its deformation space cannot cut a terminal differently.
- Mark specific: OneKey Pro 2 title, the creator build fails (`hmtx can't contain negative advance widths`: the hand hides "y " and the spacing fit drives an advance negative); Air-gap signing and with Corning Gorilla Glass have glyphs over a bright background (weight 0 on 1836 and 4669 px, "A" and "Glass"), so their IoU is computed on the visible part and the fit is weakly constrained; Mirror-polished aluminum keeps a light streak in its crop; the bluetooth icon is 14 px tall and the tracer merges its strokes (0.796).

**Next lever, already launched**: trace the text itself as free SVG paths with the F2 tracer (`F3Marks.py svg --trace-text`), which reached 0.998 on synthetic glyphs in F2; it is running detached on No worries. and OneKey (PID in `F3-trace-text.pid`, log `F3-trace-text.log`, wait with `while ps -p $(cat F3-trace-text.pid) >/dev/null; do sleep 5; done`); the result lands as the next version of `motions/onekey-f3-t-no-worries` and `motions/onekey-f3-ui-onekey`. It had not finished after 35 minutes (the free-path refinement is the known slow part). If it passes, the glyphs traced there become the per-glyph outlines of one OneKey benchmark font (one drawing per glyph, reused by every caption), which is the route to 0.99 on all captions; the title also needs a non-negative advance bound in the creator spacing stage.

